    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 031201 CCA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion \nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n450 AM PDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Drier, cooler, and breezy conditions expected through the work \n  week.\n  \n* Rain and storm chances return Saturday afternoon and Sunday as \n  monsoonal moisture surges northward\n\n* Drier conditions for Labor Day, monsoonal moisture and warmer \n  temperatures may return mid next week. \n  \n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Mid Next Week.\n\nUpper troughing persist along the west coast for at least the next \nweek keeping southwesterly flow aloft for the Desert Southwest. This \npattern supports below average temperatures and breezy conditions \nlocally into the Holiday weekend. Hi-res short term models and \nlonger range guidance both show negative 850 mb temperature \nanomalies persisting with this weather pattern, raising confidence \nthat surface temperatures will continue to run around 5 to as much \nas 10 degrees below normal... translating to highs in the 80s-90s \nfor Desert Valleys, low 100s for the CRV and Death Valley, and \ncooler in the higher terrain. In addition to cooler temperatures, \nbreezy conditions are expected through at least Saturday as mixing \ninto an enhance mid level jet brings stronger gusts down to the \nsurface. The EC EFI has been highlighting Friday and to some extent \nSaturday as the windier days within the forecast period. High \nconfidence exists in afternoon S-SW wind gusts reaching 30-40 mph \nthe next several days... with Friday likely seeing the more \nwidespread wind gusts nearing 40 mph or greater at times. Highest \nwind gusts will likely be at higher elevations or in wind prone \nvalleys. In this pattern, expect winds to remain elevated during the \novernight hours as well around 10-15 mph with isolated gusts of 20 \nmph or greater. \n\nWhile the southwesterly flow pattern will keep conditions relatively \ndry through the desert southwest as monsoonal moisture remains \nshunted to the south...Saturday afternoon into Sunday look to be the \nexception as tropical moisture is drawn northward from the East \nPacific. While the main systems will remain out at sea, confidence \nis increasing in a surge of moisture northward through the Colorado \nRiver Valley and into the Great Basin Saturday afternoon through \nSunday. Precipitable water values of at least an inch reach the I-15 \ncorridor and southern portions of Nevada by Sunday. An associated \nmid-upper shortwave moving north from the Pacific into the CRV will \nprovide enough lift to spark off showers and isolated convection \nbeginning Saturday in the CRV and Mohave County, then chances spread \nnorthward Sunday covering much of the Mojave Desert and lower Great \nBasin. Surface-based and MU CAPE looks rather marginal this weekend, \npotentially limiting the amount of convection and upscale growth of \ncells. DCAPE does not look overly impressive, but >1000j/kg values \nshould still support the possibility of gusty winds and downbursts \nwith some storms.  Still enough forcing and moisture should be in \nplace for at least numerous shower activity as the shortwave passes \nthrough the region. With increased clouds and convection in the \narea, Sunday will likely be the coolest day in the forecast period \nas highs struggle to even reach 90F in Desert Valleys. \n\nLooking toward next week, longer range guidance shows drier air \nfiltering back into the region Labor Day and Tuesday. A \nSouthwesterly flow pattern looks to somewhat remain in place next \nweek, however there are hints that the upper jet could retreat \nnorthward as ridging tries to rebuild from New Mexico westward into \nthe Mojave Desert. While Monday and Tuesday look much drier than \nSunday, watching for signals that a monsoonal pattern may be trying \nto set up again going into mid to late next week. Lower confidence \nexists in the overall details, but confidence is slowly increasing \nin a pattern supportive of monsoon activity returning, which is also \nreflected in CPC's 6-10 day precipitation outlook. CPC 6-10 day \noutlook shows no strong signals for a pattern supportive of either \nabove or below normal temperatures locally as ridging and associated \nheat remain to the east; however if the monsoon ridge does in fact \nbuild westward later next week, temperatures may also warm back to \nnear or above normal. \n\n\n\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...\nAnother night of elevated south-southwesterly wind gusts observed in \nthe Valley as a 25-35 kt low level jet remains overhead. This \npattern is expected to persist for the next 24-36+ hours, \nparticularly going into Friday as the LLJ strengthens further. While \ngusts early this morning will be more isolated with winds speeds \ndiminishing at times, expect a quick increase in wind speeds and \ngusts after 15-16z with gusts peaking at 25-30 kts. Expect winds to \ndiminish somewhat overnight, but confidence is fairly high that \nspeeds remain around 10-15 kts with isolated gusts around 20 kts \ncontinuing into Friday morning. No operationally significant cloud \ncover through the TAF period. Temperatures will remain below 100 \ndegrees.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Generally south-\nsouthwest winds continue through the TAF period, with gusts between \n20 and 30 kts. Sustained winds will remain elevated overnight \ntonight again around 10-15 kts with isolated gusts possible. The \nexception will be KDAG where gusty west winds can be expected \nthrough the TAF period, peaking late afternoon into the evening \nhours. No operationally significant cloud cover through the TAF \nperiod.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
